explanation:
This complete TypeScript example validates a simple response:
```typescript
interface Reading {
  label: string;
  amount: number;
}
function isReading(value: unknown): value is Reading {
  if (typeof value !== 'object' || value === null) return false;
  if (!('label' in value) || !('amount' in value)) return false;
  return typeof value.label === 'string'
    && typeof value.amount === 'number'
    && Number.isFinite(value.amount)
    && value.amount >= 0;
}
console.log(isReading({ label: 'Water', amount: 6 }));
console.log(isReading({ label: 'Water', amount: '6' }));
```
It prints true then false. The value is Reading return annotation is a type predicate: it tells TypeScript what a true result proves. The function body must actually check that promise.

Continue after the interface and guard:
```typescript
const incoming: unknown = { label: 'Milk', amount: 3 };
if (isReading(incoming)) {
  console.log(incoming.label);
  console.log(incoming.amount + 2);
} else {
  console.log('Invalid reading');
}
for (const amount of [-2, NaN, Infinity]) {
  console.log(isReading({ label: 'Milk', amount }));
}
```
The first branch prints Milk and 5. The loop prints false three times. Inside the true branch TypeScript permits field access because the guard has narrowed incoming.

